**Current support and accepted design.** Template directives work today. General source modifiers are accepted design queued for the general-directives plan. Entry selection uses the fixed `@entry.moth` file role, not a directive. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

A directive modifies an existing construct. Template-head directives modify their template and source directives modify the following eligible construct.

### The two locations

- In a template head, the directive applies to that template, for example `[$slot title]` or `[$md: Text]`.
- Before other source syntax, a modifier applies to the next eligible declaration or statement. `$config` is such a modifier, even when it is the first item in a file.

The compiler decides a directive's eligible target from its name, not the item's position in a file. There are no module-root directives.

### Spacing and operands

A source modifier uses its own line and must attach to the next meaningful line. Contiguous comment lines may intervene, but **an empty line between the modifier and its target is an error**.

Each directive can have at most one optional compile-time operand: `$name`, `$name value` or `$name key = value`. No authored type or function-call-style argument list is allowed.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    -- Accepted general-directive syntax:
    $config
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"
]

The current config source retains named `project` and selected-builder records, using `#Config of T` inputs until the `$config` migration. There are no `$project` or `$builder` directives. HTML metadata uses direct const `$in` slots in `@entry.moth`, whose presence makes entry eligibility explicit.

See @../directives/#directives (Directives), @../project-structure/#project-config (Project config) and @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).
